ose. So maybe safe.\n\n- Play T2: Part of the 2-3-4 chow. Discarding T2 would break that chow. If I discard T2, I can't form that chow anymore. So maybe not good.\n\n- Play W1: Characters 1. Part of 1-2-3 if I have W2 and W3. I have T2 and T3, but not W2 or W3. So maybe safe.\n\n- Play T3: Part of 2-3-4 chow. Discarding T3 would break the chow. So not ideal.\n\nSo the options that don't break potential chows are T6, F3, J1, W8, B8, W9, W5. Among these, which is the best to discard?\n\nIf I discard T6, I can keep the T2-T3-T4 chow. That's a good chow. If I discard T6, I can still form that chow. So maybe that's a good move. Alternatively, if I discard F3 or J1, but those are honor tiles. Discarding honor tiles might be less risky because they are not part of sequences. But keeping them could help in forming pungs later.\n\nBut since I have no pungs yet, maybe it's better to keep the honor tiles for possible pungs. However, since I have only one of each, discarding them might not be bad. Let's think about possible scoring patterns.\n\nMy current hand has a possible chow of T2-T3-T4. If I can form that, it's a concealed chow. Also, I have W5 (two), which could be a pair. Maybe I can aim for a hand with multiple chows and a pair. For example, if I can form T2-T3-T4 (chow), then maybe another chow with W5 as part of something. But W5 is a single tile. Alternatively, maybe I can form another chow with W8 and W9 if I get W7. But that's not likely.\n\nAlternatively, if I can get a pung of J1 or F3, but I only have one each. So maybe not. Let's check the points. If I can form a concealed hand (Fully Concealed Hand for 4 points) and have some chows. For example, if I have four chows and a pair, that's All Chows (2 points) plus Fully Concealed (4 points), totaling 6. But I need at least 8 points to win. So maybe that's not enough.\n\nWait, the minimum is 8 points. So I need to aim for a higher-scoring pattern. Let's see. What patterns can I form?\n\nLooking at my current hand: